] [17592186504650 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/98/98ed4353a10d4d3f799f984d3efdd4cacf50cc71a9171804779a7288211c065e.png)\n\nWhat element of the circle is depicted in the diagram above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504650 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "84e78e86-b0ee-5a67-bb4f-3d08e4417d49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504650 #edb/ref 1074 "We can see that $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a line segment whose endpoints lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a chord. It is not a diameter since the line segment does not pass through the center of the circle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504670 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504670 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74690" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504670 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504670 #edb/ref 1071 "What should be substituted into the blank spaces in the following sentence to make it a true statement?\n\n\"*If $A$ and $B$ are two points on a circle and $O$ is the circle's center, then $\\overset{―}{AB},$ which passes through $O,$ is $\\text{_________}$ of the circle. Additionally, if we consider the region bounded by $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and the corresponding arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB},$ then we obtain a circular $\\text{_________}.$*\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504670 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b78166bd-7c7a-5280-b285-d24358ee444d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504670 #edb/ref 1074 "First, $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a line segment that connects points on the circle and passes through the circle's center. So, $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is *a diameter* of the circle. \n\nThe region enclosed between $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and the corresponding arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB}$ is a circular *segment*.\n\nTherefore, the correct mathematical statement is the following:\n\n\"*If $A$ and $B$ are two points on a circle and $O$ is the circle's center, then $\\overset{―}{AB},$ which passes through $O,$ is **a diameter** of the circle. Additionally, if we consider the region bounded by $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and the corresponding arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB},$ then we obtain a circular **segment**.*\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504675 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504675 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102522" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504675 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504675 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1d/1dc519a8face19a4950e85b698e61e19f2a9f6d9e4ca8ecb3744707c43d4b7d3.png)\n\nWhat element of the circle is depicted in the diagram above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504675 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c18974ec-97d7-5314-ad7a-0d98ada7186c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504675 #edb/ref 1074 "We can see that $\\overset{―}{JK}$ is a line segment whose endpoints lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{JK}$ is a chord. It is not a diameter since the line segment does not pass through the center of the circle." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
